  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 200 PM CDT Thu Jul 30 2026\n\nSaturday night through Thursday:\n\nDry weather expected Sunday into Monday as high pressure settles \nover the upper Great Lakes. Temperatures begin a gradual uptrend\nearly next week as a ridge in the upper jet builds east into the\nregion. The warmth would likely peak on Tuesday as the upper ridge\naxis passes, with high temperatures in the mid to upper 80s\npossible, with dewpoints in the upper 60s to around 70. An \napproaching longwave trough will allow some diffluent flow to \noverspread the region Tuesday PM (10-20% shower/storm chances), \nintensifying Tuesday night into Wednesday as the right entrance \nof an associated jet streak passes by (25-35% shower/storm \nchances). The surface pressure response to these features is \ncertainly not looking organized on model guidance, hence our \nconfidence in rainfall with it remains low. The longwave pattern\nin the jet looks to remain predominantly zonal through next \nweek, suggesting mostly dry weather and near normal to slightly\nabove normal temperatures (highs in the low to mid 80s) for \nlate next week. That said, the proximity of the jet and smaller \nperturbations within it merit some continued ~25% precip chances\ninto Thursday. \n\nSheppard",
